            <ArticleTitle>Impact of Obesity on Robotic-Assisted Sacrocolpopexy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>36-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1553-4650(16)31154-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jmig.2016.10.012</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare operative time in women stratified by body mass index (BMI) undergoing robotic-assisted sacrocolpopexy (RASC). Secondary objectives included characterizing perioperative characteristics and reoperation rates.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective cohort study (Canadian Task Force classification II-2).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University-affiliated teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred seventy-nine consecutive patients who underwent RASC by a single surgeon from 2009 through 2013.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">RASC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of 179 patients, 61 (34%) were normal weight (BMI &lt; 25 kg/m(2)), 72 (40%) were overweight (BMI 25-30 kg/m(2)), and 46 (26%) were obese (BMI ≥ 30 kg/m(2)). Overweight patients were significantly older, more parous, more frequently postmenopausal, and more frequently underwent concomitant salpingo-oophorectomy. Median operative times were 202, 206, and 216 minutes in the normal-weight, overweight, and obese groups, respectively (p = .53).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Obese women undergoing RASC have similar operative time and procedural characteristics as normal-weight and overweight patients. Longer term outcomes are needed to ensure comparable surgical and anatomic success.</AbstractText>
